<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pros and Cons</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify complex text for peers. Communication: Mediate between peers in conflict.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can mediate simple conflicts about technology use by using gestures to indicate "balance" (e.g., hands moving like a scale to show "balance" between tech and no-tech activities). Can simplify explanations about the impact of technology by using images (e.g., pointing to a "no phone" sign when discussing screen time limits). Can use gestures and simple language to mediate discussions about the role of technology (e.g., motioning a "cut-off" hand for reducing screen time).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Display two contrasting images: one showing people actively using technology (e.g., studying on a laptop, video call) and another showing people engaged in offline activities (e.g., reading a book, playing board games). Ask students to quickly brainstorm "pros" for the first image and "cons" for the second, and vice-versa. (e.g., "Digital technology makes communication faster and easier." vs. "Too much social media can make us distracted.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a slightly complex statement about technology (e.g., "The pervasive integration of digital devices has significantly altered social interaction patterns.") into simpler terms (e.g., "Using cellphones a lot changes how we talk to friends. It can be easier to connect, but sometimes we feel less connected in person."). The teacher also models mediating a simple conflict about screen time using gestures (e.g., hands moving like a scale for "balance" between tech and no-tech activities, or a motioning a "cut-off" hand for reducing screen time), integrating modals like "You *could* try reducing screen time" or "You *should* spend time with friends."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. If I say "digital technology makes communication faster and *easier*," what does "*easier*" mean? (Target: Comparative)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. If I say "You *should* *disconnect* from *social media* sometimes," am I telling you to do it or suggesting it? (Target: Modal 'should', vocabulary 'disconnect', 'social media')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. When I move my hands like a scale, what am I showing? (Target: Gesture for 'balance' in mediation)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Present two short, contrasting opinion paragraphs (A and B) about technology, each around 3-4 sentences. These paragraphs will integrate the target vocabulary (technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively) and grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect with 'since'/'for').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Paragraph A (Pro-tech): "Digital *technology* makes communication *faster* and *easier*. We *can* *connect* with friends and family *often*, even if they are far away. I *have saved* important memories *since* I started using my *device* more *actively* for photos and videos. It is also very *helpful* for learning."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Paragraph B (Con-tech): "Sometimes, too much *social media* makes us *distracted* and less *focused* on real life. I think we *should* *reduce* our screen time. It is *more difficult* to have *meaningful* conversations when everyone is on their *cellphone*. We *could* *disconnect* more and *interact* in person for better *benefits*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discuss the positive and negative aspects of technology in a guided classroom debate, proposing solutions for overuse.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the two paragraphs and answer gist questions to identify the main points and perspectives.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Which paragraph thinks *technology* is *helpful* for learning?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Which paragraph suggests we *should* *reduce* screen time?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. According to paragraph A, how does *technology* make communication?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. What is one *solution* suggested in paragraph B to feel less *distracted*?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Modals ("can," "could," "should"), Comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), Present perfect ("since," "for"). Vocabulary: nouns (technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits), verbs (use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save), adjectives (helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused), adverbs (often, usually, actively). Pragmatic: Giving opinions, Discussing pros and cons, Offering suggestions. Pronunciation: Minimal pairs (/θ/, /ð/ sounds like "mouth" vs. "mouth," "thigh" vs. "thy"), past tense verbs ending "-ed" (/d/, /ɪd/, /t/ sounds like "saved," "disconnected," "liked").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Simplifying Statements):</b> Teacher provides 3-4 slightly complex sentences about the impact of technology (e.g., "The proliferation of interconnected devices has facilitated unprecedented global information exchange."). Students, individually, rewrite each sentence using simpler vocabulary and target grammar (modals, comparatives), focusing on clarifying the meaning for a peer. (e.g., "The *internet* helps us *interact* with people from all over the world. It *can* be *easier* to share our ideas.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Mediating Suggestions):</b> In groups of three, students receive short scenarios of mild tech-related disagreements (e.g., "Student A thinks *social media* is a waste of time, Student B thinks it's the *easiest* way to stay *connected*."). Student A states an opinion (e.g., "You *should* just *disconnect* from *social media*!"), Student B states a contrasting opinion (e.g., "No, it's *helpful*! I *have saved* many friend connections *since* I started using it *for* sharing photos."). The third student acts as a "Mediator," using modals (*could*, *should*), comparatives, and mediating phrases ("I think," "On one hand," "On the other hand," "Perhaps a *solution* *could* be...") to propose a balanced view or a *solution*, using gestures (e.g., hands for "balance" or pointing to an imaginary "no phone" sign). Focus on clear pronunciation of /θ/ and /ð/ (e.g., "think," "the") and "-ed" verb endings (e.g., "saved," "connected," "distracted").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Giving Opinions & Offering Suggestions):</b> Students work in new pairs. Each pair is given a different topic related to technology (e.g., "Cellphones in class," "Online gaming and studies," "Screen time limits at home"). Partner A expresses an opinion, Partner B expresses a contrasting opinion, and then they *take turns mediating* each other's points, using the learned language for giving opinions, discussing pros/cons, and offering suggestions. They must include at least one modal, one comparative, and try to use present perfect, along with appropriate gestures. Peers provide feedback on clarity and effective mediation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> Students engage in the 21st Century Skills Project: "Technology Use Debate". The class is divided into two teams (Pro-Technology and Anti-Technology) and a small group of designated "Mediators."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Debate Structure:</b> Each team presents their arguments (pre-prepared from previous lessons) regarding the *pros* and *cons* of *technology*, using modals ("can," "should") and comparatives ("easier," "more difficult").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediators' Role (CRITICAL Mediation Focus):</b> After each round of arguments from the debate teams, the "Mediator" group steps in. Their core role is to:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Simplify and summarize</b> the main points of *both* debate teams using simpler language for the whole class (e.g., "Team A thinks *technology* is *helpful* because it makes things *easier* like communication. Team B says it *can* make us *distracted* and *less connected*.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Clarify</b> any complex arguments made by the debaters, rephrasing them concisely.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Propose solutions</b> or middle-ground ideas that address the issues raised by *both* sides, using modals ("We *could* try...", "Students *should* consider...") and gestures (e.g., hands like a scale for "balance" between *use* and *disconnect*, or pointing to a "no phone" sign for screen time limits).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Actively *use* target vocabulary (solution, benefits, reduce, disconnect, interact, focused, distracted) and grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in simplifying and mediating, collaborative interaction among mediators and with debaters, and successful communicative task completion (clarifying, summarizing, and proposing balanced *solutions* for *technology use*).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist for the "Mediator" students, focusing on their ability to simplify, summarize, and offer solutions with appropriate language and gestures. Peer assessment sheets for the debaters could include a section for "Clarity of arguments" and "Respectful interaction," reinforcing the mediated communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Mediator Performance Observation:</b> Observe mediators during the Stage 4 debate for their ability to:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Simplify complex arguments from both teams using clear, A2.3-level language.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize *pros* and *cons* of *technology* clearly and concisely.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use appropriate modals ("can," "could," "should") and comparatives ("easier," "more difficult") to propose *solutions*.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Employ gestures (e.g., hands for "balance," pointing to a "no phone" sign) effectively to aid understanding during mediation.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integrate target vocabulary (solution, benefits, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Short Written Reflection (Exit Ticket):</b> All students complete an exit ticket with the prompt: "What is one *solution* to *balance technology use* that you learned today? How *could* it make your life *easier* or *more focused*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary, minimal pairs (/θ/, /ð/ sounds), and "-ed" endings (e.g., "saved," "disconnected").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals ("can," "could," "should") for suggestions and comparatives ("easier," "more difficult").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in simplifying, summarizing, and presenting ideas to resolve conflict or find common ground.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective and appropriate use of gestures to aid mediation and comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of target vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and respectful interaction during the mediation process.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One thing I learned about mediating conflicts about *technology use* today is...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I can use the gestures for "balance" or "cut-off" to explain how to...</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. How did using simple language and gestures help you understand or explain the *pros* and *cons* of *technology* today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What is one personal *solution* you *could* try to *balance* your own *technology use* based on today's debate?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in helping students mediate and simplify information during the debate? Were the gestures effectively integrated into the mediation tasks, especially for explaining "balance" or "reducing screen time"? Were students able to use modals ("can," "could," "should"), comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), and present perfect to propose *solutions* during the debate and mediation? What adjustments are needed for future mediation lessons, especially regarding encouraging spontaneous simplification and conflict resolution in real-world scenarios? Did the pronunciation practice (/θ/, /ð/, -ed endings) enhance clarity?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson concludes the 'Technology: Pros and Cons' theme by integrating all skills through mediation and the 21st Century Skills Project debate. The next theme will build on communication skills by exploring new scenarios that require students to apply their abilities to simplify complex ideas, express opinions, and suggest *solutions* in different contexts, preparing them for more complex real-world interactions and further development of their mediation skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>